{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reference value is zero, since there is no sure outcome (i.e., no outcome with 100% probability) in either lottery. The relevant outcomes are the losses: -6000 in Lottery A with 0.1% probability and -3000 in Lottery B with 0.2% probability. The differences from the reference value (zero) are therefore -6000 and -3000, respectively. The mean difference for each lottery is calculated as the sum of (difference × probability). For Lottery A: (-6000) × 0.001 = -6. For Lottery B: (-3000) × 0.002 = -6. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference of -6. The decision reason would therefore be indifferent between A and B, as the mean differences are equal.",
