{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The problem does not contain a sure outcome (i.e., no outcome has 100% probability), so the reference value is 0 Euros. The reason evaluates the differences between each outcome and this reference value (which are the outcomes themselves, since the reference is 0), weighted by their probabilities. For Lottery A: outcomes are 6000 (25%) and 0 (75%), so the mean difference is (6000 - 0)*0.25 + (0 - 0)*0.75 = 1500. For Lottery B: outcomes are 4000 (25%), 2000 (25%), and 0 (50%), so the mean difference is (4000 - 0)*0.25 + (2000 - 0)*0.25 + (0 - 0)*0.5 = 1000 + 500 = 1500. Thus, both lotteries have the same mean difference (1500), so the decision reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
